# Copy to .env.local for local development. Never commit .env.local.
#
# Everything here is inlined into the bundle at BUILD time by Vite, not read at
# runtime. Changing a value means rebuilding. Nothing here is secret: the Firebase web
# config identifies the project to Google's servers and is visible in any browser's
# network tab by design. Real secrets live in the backend's Secret Manager.

# ── Firebase web app ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Firebase console → Project settings (gear) → Your apps → Web app.
# If no web app is listed, add one — this is separate from the backend's service
# account and is not created by it. See apps/api/docs/FIREBASE_SETUP.md §7.
#
# Also add every origin that serves this app (localhost, and the Firebase Hosting
# domain) to Authentication → Settings → Authorized domains. A missing entry fails
# sign-in with an error that never mentions domains.
VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# ── Google Meet links ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional. Leave it empty and the "Add a Google Meet link" option on a new meeting is
# simply not offered; everything else about meetings works.
#
# Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials → the OAuth client named
# "Web client (auto created by Google Service)" → Client ID. On that same client, add
# every origin that serves this app (http://localhost:5173 and the hosting domains)
# under Authorised JavaScript origins. The Google Calendar API must be enabled, and the
# consent screen must list the calendar.events scope. See apps/api/docs/FIREBASE_SETUP.md §4.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# ── API ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave this UNSET in both development and production.
#
# The app calls a same-origin /api/v1 by default. In development the Vite dev server
# proxies that to http://localhost:8000 (see vite.config.ts); in production the
# Firebase Hosting rewrite sends it to Cloud Run (see docs/DEPLOYMENT.md). Same path,
# both environments, no CORS in production.
#
# Set it only to point a local build at a remote backend, e.g.
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://buildco-api-xxxxx.run.app/api/v1
# ...which then requires that origin to be listed in the backend's CORS_ORIGINS.
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=
